<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#placage">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plaçage</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as placées, mariages de la main gauche, left-handed marriages</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plaçage was a recognized extralegal system in the French slave colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in which European men entered into unions with women of African, Native American and mixed-race descent, known as placées. The unions, called left-handed marriages among free people of colour, were often settled by contracts that gave property to the woman and her children, and sometimes freedom if they were enslaved. It developed first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> amid a shortage of European women, was most widely practised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> and reached its height between 1769 and 1803. Emily Clark sees it as a broad range of relationships rather than a contractual system.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the French placer, to place with. Women were hard to recruit for the colonies: in 1719 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> deported 209 women felons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, and in 1713 and 1743 the authorities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> complained that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> sent unsuitable brides, so the custom of sending wives from France was dropped in the French West Indies in the mid-18th century. Many colonists came to make a fortune and return home, living meanwhile with a free woman of colour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-noir">Code Noir</a> forbade marriage between the races, but such unions continued, and young men of rank often did not marry until their late twenties or early thirties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Younger sons of noblemen, officers, planters, and wealthy merchants and administrators took placées before marriage or after a first wife died. By 1788, 1,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> women of colour and black women were maintained by white men. A married planter might keep his white family in the country and a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> for his placée and their children, often near Rampart Street, in the Faubourg Marigny or in Tremé, and arrange their education, sometimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. On his death the woman could, on legal challenge, expect up to a third of his property. Mothers negotiated settlements for their daughters, many placées ran rooming-houses or businesses, and their children formed the nucleus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gens-de-couleur-libres">gens de couleur libres</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, refugees brought a more formal plaçage and the quadroon balls to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>; in 1805 Albert Tessier, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, began twice-weekly dances for free quadroon women and white men only. Known placées include Marie Thérèse Metoyer, called Coincoin, who built a farm and ranch and bought the freedom of several of her children, and Rosette Rochon, who invested in the Faubourg Marigny and left an estate of $100,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Historian Joan Martin wrote that the women did not choose concubinage but chose to survive, and Monique Guillory saw the balls as the best of severely limited options, rooted in the value placed on light skin. After Reconstruction, the white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> historians Charles Gayarré and Alcée Fortier played down race-mixing and blamed the placées. Genetic studies find a high rate of European male haplogroups among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. The subject appears in fiction from George Washington Cable's The Grandissimes to Anne Rice's The Feast of All Saints.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Plaçage” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
